===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ere}}, {{m|enm|eare}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ēare|t=ear}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*auʀā}}, from the voiced [[Verner]] alternant of {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ausô||ear}} (compare {{cog|sco|ear}}, {{cog|fy|ear}}, {{cog|nl|oor}}, {{cog|de|Ohr}}, {{cog|sv|öra}}, {{cog|da|øre}}), from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂ṓws}} (compare {{cog|sga|áu}}, {{cog|la|auris}}, {{cog|lt|ausìs}}, {{cog|ru|у́хо}}, {{cog|sq|vesh}}, {{cog|grc|οὖς}}, {{cog|xcl|ունկն}}, and {{cog|fa|گوش|tr=gôš}}).

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q7362}}{{topics|en|Organs}} {{lb|en|countable}} The [[organ]] of [[hearing]], consisting of the [[pinna]], [[auditory canal]], [[eardrum]], [[malleus]], [[incus]], [[stapes]] and [[cochlea]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} The external part of the organ of hearing, the [[auricle]].
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=Judge Short had gone to town, and Farrar was off for a three days' cruise up the lake. I was bitterly regretting I had not gone with him when the distant notes of a coach horn reached my '''ear''', and I descried a four-in-hand winding its way up the inn road from the direction of Mohair.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|slang}} A [[police]] [[informant]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1976|author=Stirling Silliphant; Dean Riesner; Gail Morgan Hickman|title=[[w:The Enforcer (1976 movie)|The Enforcer]]
|passage=''No I'm not kidding, and if you don't give it to me I'll let it out that you’re an '''ear.'''''}}
# The sense of hearing; the perception of sounds; skill or good taste in listening to music.
#: {{ux|en|a good '''ear''' for music}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|passage=songs{{...}}not all ungrateful to thine '''ear'''}}
# The privilege of being kindly heard; favour; attention.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Apophthegmes|passage=Dionysius{{...}}would give no '''ear''' to his suit.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|III|ii|passage=Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your '''ears'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1990-08-19|quotee=Uwe Stelbrink|title=Fear and uncertainty breed xenophobia in E. Germany|journal=w:Democrat and Chronicle|location=Rochester, N.Y.|volume=158|page=5A|passage=They don’t know if they’re going to have a job in a week or a month. They don’t know if they can pay the rising prices. Instead of the paradise they expected July 1, their total existence is unsure. That some foreigners get beaten—nobody has an '''ear''' for that now.}}
# {{senseid|en|prominence or projection on an object}} That which resembles in shape or position the ear of an animal; a [[prominence]] or [[projection]] on an object, usually for [[support]] or [[attachment]]; a [[lug]]; a [[handle]]; a foot-rest or [[step]] of a [[spade]] or a similar digging tool.
#: {{cot|en|boss<id:prominence or projection on an object>|eye<id:loop or hole to receive hook or line, and integral with whole>}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''ears''' of a tub, skillet, or dish;&nbsp;&nbsp; The '''ears''' of a boat are outside kneepieces near the bow.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=291|passage=When they got as far as the little valley north of Oppenhagen - where the land-slip took place - he thought he sat between the '''ears''' of a bucket; but shortly this vanished also, and it was only then he really came to himself again.}}
# {{lb|en|architecture}} An [[acroterium]].
# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[crossette]].
# {{lb|en|journalism}} A space to the left or right of a [[publication]]'s front-page [[title]], used for [[advertising]], [[weather]], etc.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=Richard Weiner; Charles M. Levine|title=The Skinny about Best Boys, Dollies, Green Rooms, Leads, and Other Media Lingo|page=26
|passage=In journalism, '''ears''' flank the title as boxes in the left and right top corners of a publication (generally a newspaper).}}
# {{lb|en|baking}} A [[curled]] [[ridge]] in the [[crust]] of a loaf of bread where the [[dough]] was [[slashed]] before going into the [[oven]] and [[expand]]s during [[baking]].

